  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Nebraska received $1.1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 15,503 awards, with an average award size of $68.2K. Obligations were down 16.5% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show a peak of $2B in FY23, with multiple prior years at $1.3B and the current FY26 at $1.1B. The Department of Defense led buying agencies with $388M, followed by the Department of Education with $287M and the General Services Administration with $171M. Other significant agency obligations include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $101M and the Department of Agriculture at $32.5M. NELNET SERVICING LLC is the top vendor with $287M, followed by GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. with $154M. Other vendor names with notable obligations include HENDRICKSON TRANSPORTATION LLC at $43.7M and METGREEN SOLUTIONS INC at $34.7M. Computer Systems Design Services accounted for $201M in obligations, while Other Activities Related to Credit Intermediation accounted for $287M. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Nebraska federal contract spending changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 16.5% year over year. Fiscal-year totals show FY23 at $2B and FY26 to date at $1.1B. Multiple years before FY23 recorded $1.3B, indicating FY23 was an outlier peak. Current FY totals are partial and represent obligations through the fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate federal contracts in Nebraska?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense led buying agencies with $388M, followed by the Department of Education with $287M and the General Services Administration with $171M. NELNET SERVICING LLC received $287M and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. received $154M. Other major industry totals include Other Activities Related to Credit Intermediation at $287M and Computer Systems Design Services at $201M."
      }
    ]
